                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Although this is a local multiplayer game there are bots that make up for the lack of numbers. Initially Ainslie (my girlfriend) demonstrated some interest in challenging me. Unfortunately, real life events got in the way of the two of us playing and I held off writing this review. When she continued to be busy I decided to try my luck against the AI. I’m thankful that I did. This review will purely focus on the single-player experience.\n\n[h1]Strengths[/h1]\n\nVariety of vehicle types: There are a total of six. vehicle types. Each plays and performs differently from the other. Each has their own distinct advantages and disadvantages. One of the keys to being successful at the game will be discovering which vehicle you perform best with. Vehicle types include tank, Humvee, APC, artillery and hovercraft. Players also have the choice of choosing at random and having one.\n\nA ton of game modes: More game modes have been included in the game than you can poke a stick at. Game modes include: Death match, war spoils, bullet hell, future tech, bombshell, spare parts, tour of duty, arms race, experimental weapon, blitzkrieg and limited resources.  Although some play very similarly there are enough differences to provide a broad enough arrangement of game styles to appease most players.\n\nGreat tutorial system: There is a great tutorial system which can be played through multiple times for each different vehicle type. I highly recommend that you play through with each type.\n\nIn summary, thanks to a good variety of vehicle types, game modes and a very well designed tutorial system that can be - and should be - replayed Toys of War's strengths make the game a ton of fun.\n\n[h1]Weaknesses[/h1]\n\nMouse play: The biggest actual weakness in the game is the mouse play. The game has been designed with a controller in mind and with twin stick shooting mechanics at its heart. Therefore, unfortunately, the mouse play is vastly inferior to controller play.\n\nMarketing: Unfortunately, when a game has been released for over a week, as of writing, and the game has just the sole review on steam marketing is to blame. In this respect the failure to mark the game as a single-player game is a significant hindrance. I understand that the developers have not wanted to market a game intended for local multiplayer unfairly. However, players such as myself enjoy the single-player options that the game has. Yes, I’m missing fun with friends but I do get joy from the game.\n\nSlow loading time at the beginning: The original loading screen took my computer quite some time to process. This is a little disappointing given that there doesn’t appear to be a lot of content loading. However, the menu screens are quite well detailed and polished. Therefore, if this is the reason why then I can and will deal with it.\n\n[h1]Is it Steamified?[/h1]\n\nToys of War already has a number of Steam achievements for the hunters out there. However, unfortunately, it currently lacks trading cards. The developers know that adding them will spur sales so I hope that they do so.\n\n[h1]Verdict[/h1]\n\nAlthough, Toys of War, would make for a highly entertaining party game I don’t feel as though I missed out on the experience as a single-player game. Unfortunately, because the game is marked as a local multiplayer game and not as a single-player the game has been ignored by most on Steam. Those who love action games and arena games would find this highly enjoyable.\n\nAs a single player, I recommend this if you’re looking for an arena shooter and are looking for some solidly designed AI. The great thing about this game is that it will never be dead and you’ll always be able to play it with or without other players around.\n\nFor similar reviews and our popular giveaways (including hardware) please consider checking out [url=http://www.steamified.com/]Steamified.com[/url]",
